 *  Maximum edge-disjoint S->T paths after reversing exactly one directed edge.
 *
 *  Idea:
 *  - Each road has capacity 1.
 *  - Current answer = max-flow from S to T.
 *  - By max-flow/min-cut, after computing one maximum flow, in the residual graph:
 *      A = vertices reachable from S
 *      B = vertices from which T is reachable (equiv. reachable from T in reverse residual graph)
 *    Let C = V \ (A ∪ B).
 *
 *  Known characterization:
 *  - Reversing one edge can increase the max-flow by at most 1.
 *  - It increases by 1 iff the reversed edge is from a vertex in B to a vertex in A
 *    in the ORIGINAL graph.
 *
 *  Counting roads achieving the increase:
 *  - Any original edge u->v with u in B and v in A works.
 *  - New maximum = old maximum + 1 if at least one such edge exists, else old maximum.
 *  - Number of roads = count of such edges.
 *
 *  Why:
 *  Reversing u->v (u in B, v in A) creates residual connectivity S ~> v, then reversed edge v->u,
 *  then u ~> T, thus an augmenting path appears in modified network.
 *  Conversely, if one reversal increases flow, the changed residual augmenting path implies the
 *  reversed edge must connect B to A.
 *
 *  Input format assumed:
 *      N M S T
 *      u1 v1
 *      ...
 *      uM vM
